Description: close the onward connection when a login through the pipe fails
 sshpiperd makes a new connection to the upstream (onward) SSH server for
 every authentication attempt of a downstream client, and authenticates to
 it with what the client gave. When that failed, the connection was never
 closed:
 .
  - authUpstream returned on a failed clientAuthenticateReturnAllowed
    without closing the connection it had just made. Each wrong password
    relayed to the upstream sshd therefore left one unauthenticated
    connection there until that sshd's own LoginGraceTime (120 s by
    default), holding one of its MaxStartups slots (10:30:100 by default).
    A dozen wrong passwords through the proxy shut real logins out of the
    upstream ("drop connection #11 ... Maxstartups" in its log).
  - NewSSHPiperConn returned an error when the downstream client did not
    finish authenticating (it went away, or offered a public key that a
    plugin maps to an upstream private key, and never signed for it) without
    closing an upstream connection a callback had already authenticated.
    That one stayed open, authenticated, with nothing piped to it.
  - a second successful callback on one downstream connection replaced
    p.upstream without closing the first.
 .
 The patch closes the upstream connection in those three places and nowhere
 else. It is safe for retries: a failed upstream connection is local to
 authUpstream and is never reused, since the next attempt of the same
 downstream client calls its callback again, which dials a new one. So the
 client keeps its normal password attempts within one connection. A
 connection that authenticated and is then piped is untouched: it is only
 closed when no pipe will ever be made from it.
 .
 Not changed: how keys are relayed, and sshpiperd's own handling of its
 login grace time.
 .
 Tested by packaging/install-test.sh in a clean container of each suite:
 12 wrong passwords through sshpiperd to a throwaway sshd, each in its own
 connection, then no established TCP connection may remain at that sshd's
 port, and all 12 must have been refused by the sshd itself (none dropped
 for MaxStartups); then one connection with a wrong password followed by
 the right one must log in, one connection must get three attempts, and a
 correct login must still work afterwards. Without the patch the test fails
 with 11 connections left open and the twelfth dropped by MaxStartups.
 .
 The module's own tests (go test ./ssh -run Piper) pass with the patch except
 TestPiperUsernameNotChangedWithinSession, whose fake upstream server now
 reports "cannot start upstream [ssh: no auth passed yet, access denied]":
 its upstreams refuse the relayed credentials, and the test helper counts an
 upstream that sees its connection closed during authentication as an error.
 Before the patch those fake upstreams simply never returned, which is the
 leak. The test's assertions about the client and the piper still hold. The
 test file is not changed here: it is not part of what is built.
Author: fpgas.online packaging (https://github.com/fpgas-online/sshpiper)
Origin: vendor
Forwarded: no (kept in fpgas-online/sshpiper only)
Applies-To: github.com/tg123/sshpiper.crypto v0.57.0-sshpiper-20260929
 (the golang.org/x/crypto that cmd/sshpiperd/go.mod replaces), file
 ssh/sshpiper.go
---
--- a/ssh/sshpiper.go
+++ b/ssh/sshpiper.go
@@ -332,6 +332,12 @@
 	}
 
 	if err := u.clientAuthenticateReturnAllowed(config); err != nil {
+		// This upstream connection is never used again: a retry by the
+		// downstream client gets a new one from its callback. Left open,
+		// it stays unauthenticated at the upstream server until that
+		// server's own login timeout.
+		u.transport.Close()
+
 		if p.config.UpstreamAuthFailureCallback != nil {
 			p.config.UpstreamAuthFailureCallback(downstream, method, err, p.challengeCtx)
 		}
@@ -342,6 +348,12 @@
 	}
 
 	u.user = config.User
+	if p.upstream != nil {
+		// An earlier callback of this downstream connection already
+		// authenticated to an upstream (a public key that was offered and
+		// accepted, but not yet signed for). Only one is ever piped.
+		p.upstream.transport.Close()
+	}
 	p.upstream = u
 
 	return nil
@@ -487,6 +499,14 @@
 	}
 
 	if err := p.mapToUpstreamViaDownstreamAuth(); err != nil {
+		if p.upstream != nil {
+			// The downstream client did not finish authenticating (it
+			// went away, or offered a public key and never signed for
+			// it), but a callback had already authenticated to the
+			// upstream: nothing will ever be piped to that connection.
+			p.upstream.transport.Close()
+			p.upstream = nil
+		}
 		return nil, err
 	}
 
